-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="9ccdc6c2-369f-4994-ac76-724e7258cb4b" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="316">Got it — here’s a <strong data-start="18" data-end="66">consolidated timeline and cast of characters</strong> written as if for the opening appendix or background chapter of your <em data-start="136" data-end="157">Viewpoint Diversity</em> thesis. It integrates your provided sources into a single, coherent narrative, stretching from the philosophical prehistory to the formalization of the CTM.</p>
<hr data-start="318" data-end="321">
<h1 data-start="323" data-end="391"><strong data-start="325" data-end="391">Timeline of Consciousness Studies and the Emergence of the CTM</strong></h1>
<h3 data-start="393" data-end="452"><strong data-start="397" data-end="450">Pre-1990: Philosophical and Theological Dominance</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="453" data-end="600">
<li data-start="453" data-end="521">
<p data-start="455" data-end="521">Consciousness primarily treated by philosophers and theologians.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="522" data-end="600">
<p data-start="524" data-end="600">Scientific study of subjective experience considered speculative or taboo.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="602" data-end="605">
<h3 data-start="607" data-end="642"><strong data-start="611" data-end="640">1990: The Scientific Turn</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="643" data-end="969">
<li data-start="643" data-end="786">
<p data-start="645" data-end="786"><strong data-start="645" data-end="665">fMRI development</strong>: Non-invasive observation of brain function becomes possible, shifting neuroscience toward studying subjective states.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="787" data-end="969">
<p data-start="789" data-end="969"><strong data-start="789" data-end="807">Daniel Dennett</strong>, <em data-start="809" data-end="834">Consciousness Explained</em> (1990): Functionalist and computationalist framework, presenting consciousness as no more mysterious than other cognitive functions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="971" data-end="974">
<h3 data-start="976" data-end="1021"><strong data-start="980" data-end="1019">Early–Mid 1990s: Breaking the Taboo</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1022" data-end="1330">
<li data-start="1022" data-end="1171">
<p data-start="1024" data-end="1171"><strong data-start="1024" data-end="1041">Francis Crick</strong>, <em data-start="1043" data-end="1071">The Astonishing Hypothesis</em> (1994): Advocates scientific study of “neurocorrelates of consciousness,” legitimizing the field.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1172" data-end="1330">
<p data-start="1174" data-end="1330"><strong data-start="1174" data-end="1192">David Chalmers</strong>, <em data-start="1194" data-end="1214">The Conscious Mind</em> (1996): Introduces the <strong data-start="1238" data-end="1254">Hard Problem</strong> of subjective experience versus “easy” computational/functional problems.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1332" data-end="1335">
<h3 data-start="1337" data-end="1381"><strong data-start="1341" data-end="1379">1990s–2000s: Foundational Theories</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1382" data-end="2142">
<li data-start="1382" data-end="1557">
<p data-start="1384" data-end="1557"><strong data-start="1384" data-end="1401">Bernard Baars</strong>, <em data-start="1403" data-end="1428">Global Workspace Theory</em> (GWT): The “theater of consciousness” metaphor; a small “stage” (STM) with information broadcast to a larger “audience” (LTM).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1558" data-end="1710">
<p data-start="1560" data-end="1710"><strong data-start="1560" data-end="1575">Alan Turing</strong> (retrospectively): Universal Turing Machine as formal model for computation → later inspires the <strong data-start="1673" data-end="1707">Conscious Turing Machine (CTM)</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1711" data-end="1851">
<p data-start="1713" data-end="1851"><strong data-start="1713" data-end="1733">Michael Graziano</strong>, <em data-start="1735" data-end="1760">Attention Schema Theory</em>: Consciousness arises from the brain’s internal sketch of its own attentional processes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1852" data-end="1996">
<p data-start="1854" data-end="1996"><strong data-start="1854" data-end="1870">Karl Friston</strong>, <em data-start="1872" data-end="1919">Predictive Processing / Free Energy Principle</em>: Brain as a prediction-error minimizer via hierarchical generative models.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1997" data-end="2142">
<p data-start="1999" data-end="2142"><strong data-start="1999" data-end="2015">Eva Jablonka</strong> and evolutionary theorists: Consciousness understood as adaptation; workspace functions seen as evolutionarily advantageous.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2144" data-end="2147">
<h3 data-start="2149" data-end="2203"><strong data-start="2153" data-end="2201">2000s–2010s: Cross-Pollination and Conflicts</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="2204" data-end="2467">
<li data-start="2204" data-end="2345">
<p data-start="2206" data-end="2345"><strong data-start="2206" data-end="2236">Adversarial collaborations</strong> between GWT and IIT (Tononi): efforts to test predictions of workspace vs. information-integration models.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2346" data-end="2467">
<p data-start="2348" data-end="2467"><strong data-start="2348" data-end="2366">Kevin Mitchell</strong> (2010s): poses 15 “multi-questions” outlining desiderata for any complete theory of consciousness.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2469" data-end="2472">
<h3 data-start="2474" data-end="2517"><strong data-start="2478" data-end="2515">2010s–2020s: Toward Formal Models</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="2518" data-end="3254">
<li data-start="2518" data-end="2639">
<p data-start="2520" data-end="2639"><strong data-start="2520" data-end="2562">Aram Blum’s Sleeping Experts Algorithm</strong>: Weighted expert advice model, later integrated into CTM’s LTM processors.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2640" data-end="2725">
<p data-start="2642" data-end="2725"><strong data-start="2642" data-end="2654">Brainish</strong>: Development of CTM’s self-generated multimodal internal “language.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2726" data-end="2832">
<p data-start="2728" data-end="2832"><strong data-start="2728" data-end="2755">Formal CTM architecture</strong>: Seven-tuple structure (STM, LTM, uptree, downtree, links, input, output).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2833" data-end="2935">
<p data-start="2835" data-end="2935"><strong data-start="2835" data-end="2864">Probabilistic competition</strong>: Introduction of “coin flip neurons” to make competition stochastic.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2936" data-end="3040">
<p data-start="2938" data-end="3040"><strong data-start="2938" data-end="2964">Theorem of competition</strong>: Winning probability depends on relative weights, not processor location.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3041" data-end="3145">
<p data-start="3043" data-end="3145"><strong data-start="3043" data-end="3073">Axiom 1 (Global Broadcast)</strong>: Winning chunk broadcast to all LTM processors → conscious attention.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3146" data-end="3254">
<p data-start="3148" data-end="3254"><strong data-start="3148" data-end="3172">Axiom 2 (Inspection)</strong>: Conscious awareness arises when a broadcasted chunk is inspected and unpacked.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3256" data-end="3259">
<h3 data-start="3261" data-end="3303"><strong data-start="3265" data-end="3301">Recent Developments (~2023–2025)</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="3304" data-end="3781">
<li data-start="3304" data-end="3423">
<p data-start="3306" data-end="3423"><strong data-start="3306" data-end="3338">Prediction of pain asymbolia</strong>: CTM forecasts multiple distinct types depending on labeling of “self” and “pain.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3424" data-end="3524">
<p data-start="3426" data-end="3524"><strong data-start="3426" data-end="3452">Key-Value Memory paper</strong>: Links between transformer architectures and CTM’s Brainish encoding.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3525" data-end="3649">
<p data-start="3527" data-end="3649"><strong data-start="3527" data-end="3574">Monograph: <em data-start="3540" data-end="3572">AI Consciousness is Inevitable</em></strong> (forthcoming, Cambridge UP): The Blum team’s full exposition of the CTM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3650" data-end="3781">
<p data-start="3652" data-end="3781"><strong data-start="3652" data-end="3660">Talk</strong>: Lenore, Manuel, and Avrim Blum, <em data-start="3694" data-end="3748">A Theoretical Computer Science Lens on Consciousness</em> (YouTube, ~1h10m + Q&amp;A, 2025).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3783" data-end="3786">
<h1 data-start="3788" data-end="3812"><strong data-start="3790" data-end="3812">Cast of Characters</strong></h1>
<ul data-start="3814" data-end="5075">
<li data-start="3814" data-end="3913">
<p data-start="3816" data-end="3913"><strong data-start="3816" data-end="3831">Lenore Blum</strong> — Theoretical computer scientist; co-creator of CTM; presenter of CTM in talks.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3914" data-end="3995">
<p data-start="3916" data-end="3995"><strong data-start="3916" data-end="3931">Manuel Blum</strong> — Turing Award–winning computer scientist; co-creator of CTM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3996" data-end="4100">
<p data-start="3998" data-end="4100"><strong data-start="3998" data-end="4012">Avrim Blum</strong> — Machine learning expert; co-creator of CTM; developed <em data-start="4069" data-end="4097">Sleeping Experts Algorithm</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4101" data-end="4196">
<p data-start="4103" data-end="4196"><strong data-start="4103" data-end="4121">Daniel Dennett</strong> — Philosopher; functionalist approach; <em data-start="4161" data-end="4186">Consciousness Explained</em> (1990).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4197" data-end="4313">
<p data-start="4199" data-end="4313"><strong data-start="4199" data-end="4216">Francis Crick</strong> — Biologist; <em data-start="4230" data-end="4258">The Astonishing Hypothesis</em> (1994); advocated scientific study of consciousness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4314" data-end="4389">
<p data-start="4316" data-end="4389"><strong data-start="4316" data-end="4334">David Chalmers</strong> — Philosopher; introduced the “Hard Problem” (1996).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4390" data-end="4471">
<p data-start="4392" data-end="4471"><strong data-start="4392" data-end="4407">Alan Turing</strong> — Mathematician; Universal Turing Machine as CTM inspiration.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4472" data-end="4570">
<p data-start="4474" data-end="4570"><strong data-start="4474" data-end="4491">Bernard Baars</strong> — Psychologist; Global Workspace Theory, CTM’s primary structural precursor.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4571" data-end="4670">
<p data-start="4573" data-end="4670"><strong data-start="4573" data-end="4593">Michael Graziano</strong> — Neuroscientist; Attention Schema Theory; self-modeling as consciousness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4671" data-end="4758">
<p data-start="4673" data-end="4758"><strong data-start="4673" data-end="4689">Karl Friston</strong> — Neuroscientist; Free Energy Principle and predictive processing.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4759" data-end="4842">
<p data-start="4761" data-end="4842"><strong data-start="4761" data-end="4777">Eva Jablonka</strong> — Evolutionary theorist; workspace as evolutionary adaptation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4843" data-end="4944">
<p data-start="4845" data-end="4944"><strong data-start="4845" data-end="4863">Kevin Mitchell</strong> — Neurogeneticist; posed 15 essential questions for theories of consciousness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4945" data-end="5075">
<p data-start="4947" data-end="5075"><strong data-start="4947" data-end="4973">Greyhack, Klein, Jiren</strong> — Scholars engaged in debates on pain asymbolia; their critiques led CTM to predict multiple types.</p>
</li>
</ul>
