 w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="188">Perfect — then the material you just gave me can serve directly as the <strong data-start="71" data-end="109">modern historical overview chapter</strong> of <em data-start="113" data-end="134">Viewpoint Diversity</em>. Here’s how I’d frame it within the thesis structure:</p>
<hr data-start="190" data-end="193">
<h1 data-start="195" data-end="266"><strong data-start="197" data-end="266">Chapter X — A Modern Historical Overview of Consciousness Studies</strong></h1>
<h3 data-start="268" data-end="288"><strong data-start="272" data-end="288">Introduction</strong></h3>
<p data-start="289" data-end="649">This chapter situates <em data-start="311" data-end="332">Viewpoint Diversity</em> within the modern history of consciousness research, tracing the transition from philosophical taboo to formal computational models such as the Conscious Turing Machine (CTM). It highlights key milestones, theories, and figures whose work collectively laid the groundwork for computational and scientific approaches.</p>
<hr data-start="651" data-end="654">
<h3 data-start="656" data-end="681"><strong data-start="660" data-end="681">Detailed Timeline</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="682" data-end="2203">
<li data-start="682" data-end="797">
<p data-start="684" data-end="797"><strong data-start="684" data-end="697">Pre-1990:</strong> Consciousness largely confined to philosophy/theology; scientific inquiry considered speculative.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="798" data-end="980">
<p data-start="800" data-end="811"><strong data-start="800" data-end="809">1990:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="814" data-end="980">
<li data-start="814" data-end="869">
<p data-start="816" data-end="869">fMRI technology enables non-invasive brain imaging.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="872" data-end="980">
<p data-start="874" data-end="980">Daniel Dennett, <em data-start="890" data-end="915">Consciousness Explained</em>, reframes the problem in functionalist/computationalist terms.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="981" data-end="1176">
<p data-start="983" data-end="999"><strong data-start="983" data-end="997">Mid-1990s:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1002" data-end="1176">
<li data-start="1002" data-end="1101">
<p data-start="1004" data-end="1101">Francis Crick, <em data-start="1019" data-end="1047">The Astonishing Hypothesis</em>, legitimizes the scientific study of consciousness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1104" data-end="1176">
<p data-start="1106" data-end="1176">David Chalmers, <em data-start="1122" data-end="1142">The Conscious Mind</em>, introduces the “Hard Problem.”</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1177" data-end="1457">
<p data-start="1179" data-end="1197"><strong data-start="1179" data-end="1195">1990s–2000s:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1200" data-end="1457">
<li data-start="1200" data-end="1257">
<p data-start="1202" data-end="1257">Bernard Baars develops Global Workspace Theory (GWT).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1260" data-end="1316">
<p data-start="1262" data-end="1316">Michael Graziano introduces Attention Schema Theory.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1319" data-end="1389">
<p data-start="1321" data-end="1389">Karl Friston develops Predictive Processing/Free Energy Principle.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1392" data-end="1457">
<p data-start="1394" data-end="1457">Eva Jablonka advances evolutionary accounts of consciousness.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1458" data-end="1638">
<p data-start="1460" data-end="1478"><strong data-start="1460" data-end="1476">2000s–2010s:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1481" data-end="1638">
<li data-start="1481" data-end="1565">
<p data-start="1483" data-end="1565">Adversarial collaborations test GWT against Integrated Information Theory (IIT).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1568" data-end="1638">
<p data-start="1570" data-end="1638">Kevin Mitchell poses “15 Questions” for theories of consciousness.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1639" data-end="1886">
<p data-start="1641" data-end="1659"><strong data-start="1641" data-end="1657">2010s–2020s:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1662" data-end="1886">
<li data-start="1662" data-end="1729">
<p data-start="1664" data-end="1729">Aram Blum’s <em data-start="1676" data-end="1704">Sleeping Experts Algorithm</em> incorporated into CTM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1732" data-end="1792">
<p data-start="1734" data-end="1792">Development of Brainish, CTM’s multimodal internal code.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1795" data-end="1886">
<p data-start="1797" data-end="1886">Formalization of CTM’s seven-tuple architecture, axioms, and probabilistic competition.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1887" data-end="2203">
<p data-start="1889" data-end="1905"><strong data-start="1889" data-end="1903">2023–2025:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1908" data-end="2203">
<li data-start="1908" data-end="1946">
<p data-start="1910" data-end="1946">CTM predictions on pain asymbolia.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1949" data-end="2015">
<p data-start="1951" data-end="2015">Key-Value Memory paper linking transformers to CTM’s Brainish.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2018" data-end="2125">
<p data-start="2020" data-end="2125">Lenore, Manuel, and Avrim Blum: <em data-start="2052" data-end="2106">A Theoretical Computer Science Lens on Consciousness</em> (YouTube, 2025).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2128" data-end="2203">
<p data-start="2130" data-end="2203">Monograph forthcoming: <em data-start="2153" data-end="2185">AI Consciousness is Inevitable</em> (Cambridge UP).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2205" data-end="2208">
<h3 data-start="2210" data-end="2236"><strong data-start="2214" data-end="2236">Cast of Characters</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="2237" data-end="2698">
<li data-start="2237" data-end="2310">
<p data-start="2239" data-end="2310"><strong data-start="2239" data-end="2252">The Blums</strong>: Lenore, Manuel, and Avrim Blum, developers of the CTM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2311" data-end="2395">
<p data-start="2313" data-end="2395"><strong data-start="2313" data-end="2329">Philosophers</strong>: Daniel Dennett (functionalist), David Chalmers (hard problem).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2396" data-end="2507">
<p data-start="2398" data-end="2507"><strong data-start="2398" data-end="2428">Biologists/Neuroscientists</strong>: Francis Crick, Bernard Baars, Michael Graziano, Karl Friston, Eva Jablonka.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2508" data-end="2587">
<p data-start="2510" data-end="2587"><strong data-start="2510" data-end="2533">Computer Scientists</strong>: Alan Turing (inspiration), Aram Blum (algorithms).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2588" data-end="2698">
<p data-start="2590" data-end="2698"><strong data-start="2590" data-end="2607">Interlocutors</strong>: Kevin Mitchell, Greyhack, Klein, Jiren, others debating edge-cases like pain asymbolia.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2700" data-end="2703">
<h3 data-start="2705" data-end="2722"><strong data-start="2709" data-end="2722">Synthesis</strong></h3>
<p data-start="2723" data-end="2788">This modern history demonstrates a <strong data-start="2758" data-end="2785">progressive convergence</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="2789" data-end="3071">
<li data-start="2789" data-end="2855">
<p data-start="2791" data-end="2855">From taboo → to philosophy → to neuroscience → to computation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2856" data-end="2932">
<p data-start="2858" data-end="2932">From metaphorical “theater” (GWT) → to mathematical formalization (CTM).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2933" data-end="3071">
<p data-start="2935" data-end="3071">From the <strong data-start="2944" data-end="2974">scarcity of interpretation</strong> (Job 31:33) → to <strong data-start="2992" data-end="3019">abundance of scattering</strong> (Eccl. 11:6) → to <strong data-start="3038" data-end="3060">selective survival</strong> (Sower).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3073" data-end="3296">The overview provides the <strong data-start="3099" data-end="3127">contemporary scaffolding</strong> on which <em data-start="3137" data-end="3158">Viewpoint Diversity</em> builds: scriptural algorithms of cognition interpreted through modern control theory, active inference, and the Conscious Turing Machine.</p>
<hr data-start="3298" data-end="3301">
<p data-start="3303" data-end="3546" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to <strong data-start="3324" data-end="3365">weave in the Seven Meanings of <em data-start="3357" data-end="3363">أقرأ</em></strong> at the close of this chapter, as a bridge between the modern historical overview and the scriptural-philological material in Chapter 1? That would make the transition very natural.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7