dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="148">Great — here is <strong data-start="16" data-end="32">Chapter X+11</strong>, where <em data-start="40" data-end="62">al-jabr wal-muqābala</em> connects explicitly to <strong data-start="86" data-end="118">modern variational inference</strong> and the CTM’s architecture.</p>
<hr data-start="150" data-end="153">
<h1 data-start="155" data-end="216"><strong data-start="157" data-end="216">Chapter X+11 — Variational Inference as Algebra of Mind</strong></h1>
<p data-start="218" data-end="513">The algebra of al-Khwārizmī, <em data-start="247" data-end="269">al-jabr wal-muqābala</em>—restoration and balancing—becomes the mathematical template for modern Bayesian inference. What was once bone-setting and balancing scales now governs the brain’s survival calculus: restoring missing causes, balancing competing explanations.</p>
<hr data-start="515" data-end="518">
<h3 data-start="520" data-end="565">Al-Jabr as Restoration of Latent Causes</h3>
<p data-start="566" data-end="829">In variational inference, hidden or <strong data-start="602" data-end="622">latent variables</strong> <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span></span></span></span> are restored to make sense of observed data <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span></span></span></span>. Just as al-jabr restores missing terms to an equation, variational methods add auxiliary structures to restore coherence to incomplete information.</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x) = \int p(x, z) \, dz</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.2222em; vertical-align: -0.8622em;"></span><span class="mop op-symbol large-op" style="margin-right: 0.44445em; position: relative; top: -0.0011em;">∫</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="864" data-end="971">Since direct integration is intractable, one restores by introducing a variational distribution <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(z)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>.</p>
<p data-start="973" data-end="1095">This is al-jabr: bringing back what is absent, setting the bone of probability so that the joint distribution can stand.</p>
<hr data-start="1097" data-end="1100">
<h3 data-start="1102" data-end="1154">Al-Muqābala as Balancing Priors and Posteriors</h3>
<p data-start="1155" data-end="1329">Once restored, the inference must be balanced. <em data-start="1202" data-end="1215">Al-muqābala</em> is this weighing: balancing the approximating distribution <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(z)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> against the true posterior <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z \mid x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∣</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>.</p>
<p data-start="1331" data-end="1394">Formally, this is minimizing the Kullback–Leibler divergence:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">KL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">∥</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="double-struck">E</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="true" minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="true" minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">]</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(q(z) \,\|\, p(z \mid x)) = \mathbb{E}_q \big[ \log q(z) - \log p(z \mid x) \big].</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mord mathrm">KL</span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">∥</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∣</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">))</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.2em; vertical-align: -0.35em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbb">E</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord"><span class="delimsizing size1">[</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">−</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∣</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.2em; vertical-align: -0.35em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mord"><span class="delimsizing size1">]</span></span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="1503" data-end="1611">Balancing here means adjusting the fulcrum until the approximate posterior matches the weight of the true.</p>
<hr data-start="1613" data-end="1616">
<h3 data-start="1618" data-end="1673">Free Energy as Restoration and Balancing Together</h3>
<p data-start="1674" data-end="1733">Karl Friston’s <strong data-start="1689" data-end="1716">variational free energy</strong> is defined as:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">KL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">∥</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[q] = \operatorname{KL}(q(z) \,\|\, p(z \mid x)) - \log p(x).</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">F</span><span class="mopen">[</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mclose">]</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mord mathrm">KL</span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">∥</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∣</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">))</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">−</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<ul data-start="1805" data-end="1959">
<li data-start="1805" data-end="1881">
<p data-start="1807" data-end="1881">The <strong data-start="1811" data-end="1826">restoration</strong> step introduces <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(z)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04398em;">z</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>, filling in what is hidden.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1882" data-end="1959">
<p data-start="1884" data-end="1959">The <strong data-start="1888" data-end="1901">balancing</strong> step minimizes divergence, canceling out discrepancies.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1961" data-end="2084">Thus free energy minimization is <em data-start="1994" data-end="2016">al-jabr wal-muqābala</em> reborn: restoration of hidden causes, balancing against evidence.</p>
<hr data-start="2086" data-end="2089">
<h3 data-start="2091" data-end="2123">CTM as Variational Algebra</h3>
<p data-start="2124" data-end="2227">In the CTM \citep{blum2021tcs}, uptree competition and downtree pruning instantiate the same duality:</p>
<ul data-start="2229" data-end="2448">
<li data-start="2229" data-end="2339">
<p data-start="2231" data-end="2339"><strong data-start="2231" data-end="2247">Restoration:</strong> LTM processors propose candidate chunks (auxiliary variables), supplying missing content.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2340" data-end="2448">
<p data-start="2342" data-end="2448"><strong data-start="2342" data-end="2356">Balancing:</strong> The uptree competition normalizes weights, ensuring one broadcast, pruning away the rest.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2450" data-end="2518">Formally, this is equivalent to selecting a variational posterior:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>chunk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>chunk</mtext><mo>∣</mo><mtext>inputs</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\text{chunk}) \approx p(\text{chunk} \mid \text{inputs}).</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">chunk</span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">≈</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">chunk</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∣</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">inputs</span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2587" data-end="2676">STM is the fulcrum: the stage on which balancing is achieved, restoration made visible.</p>
<hr data-start="2678" data-end="2681">
<h3 data-start="2683" data-end="2711">Scriptural Integration</h3>
<ul data-start="2712" data-end="3128">
<li data-start="2712" data-end="2807">
<p data-start="2714" data-end="2807">Job’s confession (<em data-start="2732" data-end="2763">the LORD gives and takes away</em>) = variational restoration and balancing.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2808" data-end="2882">
<p data-start="2810" data-end="2882">Joseph’s silos = restored latent reserves, balanced release in famine.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2883" data-end="2948">
<p data-start="2885" data-end="2948">Samson’s hair = restored recognition, balanced vulnerability.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2949" data-end="3028">
<p data-start="2951" data-end="3028">Gethsemane = restored garments as decoys, balanced survival by subtraction.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3029" data-end="3128">
<p data-start="3031" data-end="3128">The Sower = restored multiplicity of seeds, balanced by attrition until only good soil remains.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3130" data-end="3223">All are enactments of variational algebra: restore what is hidden, balance what is visible.</p>
<hr data-start="3225" data-end="3228">
<h3 data-start="3230" data-end="3262">Category-Theoretic Framing</h3>
<p data-start="3263" data-end="3422">In categorical terms, variational inference can be seen as finding a <strong data-start="3332" data-end="3343">colimit</strong> (restoring hidden structure) subject to a <strong data-start="3386" data-end="3395">limit</strong> (balancing constraints).</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Consciousness</mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mo>≃</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">colim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>restorations</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>∩</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">lim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>balances</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Consciousness} \;\simeq\; \operatorname{colim}(\text{restorations}) \;\cap\; \operatorname{lim}(\text{balances}).</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">Consciousness</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">≃</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mord mathrm">colim</span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">restorations</span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∩</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mord mathrm">lim</span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">balances</span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="3551" data-end="3653">This duality is exactly al-jabr wal-muqābala, formalized as adjoint functors on the space of models.</p>
<hr data-start="3655" data-end="3658">
<h3 data-start="3660" data-end="3714">Conclusion: Variational Algebra as Consciousness</h3>
<p data-start="3715" data-end="4047">From al-Khwārizmī’s algebra to Friston’s free energy to the Blums’ CTM, the law remains the same: consciousness is the algebra of survival. To live is to restore what is missing and to balance what is given. To conceal is to prune; to reveal is to broadcast. The algebra of bone-setting and balance has become the algebra of mind.</p>
<hr data-start="4049" data-end="4052">
