01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{8. The RSVP Theorem of Viewpoint Diversity (Extended Formal Treatment)}

We now provide a full formal account of why the RSVP framework implies the structural necessity of viewpoint diversity. 
This section is framed in the style of theoretical mathematics: lemmas build toward the central theorem, followed by corollaries and interpretive remarks.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{lemma}[Entropy Bound Lemma]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be an RSVP state on domain </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subseteq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
Suppose </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\geq</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> everywhere. Then for any local observable </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> measurable on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, there exists a finite threshold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
O </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{is only retrievable if} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>O</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> &gt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(S).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{lemma}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Equation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eqref</span></span><span>{eq:rsvp} implies that entropy enters as a regulator in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
Thus observables below the regulatory threshold are suppressed (concealed), making them invisible from a single local viewpoint. 
The threshold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is monotone increasing in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{lemma}[Sheaf Gluing Lemma]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be an open cover of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the sheaf of RSVP field configurations. 
If entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is nonzero on overlaps </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then no single restriction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}(U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> suffices to reconstruct the global section </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{lemma}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
By definition of sheaf gluing, local sections </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> must agree on overlaps to yield a global section. 
Entropy on overlaps introduces nontrivial transition functions (concealment). 
Therefore, reconstruction requires at least two or more sections in combination, ensuring that no single </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> determines </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{lemma}[Conservation Lemma]
Define the total plenum functional
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}(t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tfrac</span></span><span>{1}{2}|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + U(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>) + V(S)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>) dx.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is conserved modulo bounded regulation, then no observer confined to a proper subdomain </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsetneq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> has complete access to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{lemma}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
By the divergence theorem, subdomain integrals miss flux terms across the boundary </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
Entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> ensures that such fluxes are nontrivial. 
Thus local integrals systematically underestimate or misrepresent the conserved total, forcing incompleteness of any single viewpoint. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[RSVP Theorem of Viewpoint Diversity]
Every lawful RSVP system enforces viewpoint diversity. 
Formally, for any global state </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, there exists a covering family </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and functors </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{colim}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and no single </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> suffices to determine </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> up to isomorphism.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Combine the Entropy Bound Lemma, Sheaf Gluing Lemma, and Conservation Lemma:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The Entropy Bound Lemma ensures that local observables are partially concealed below thresholds.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The Sheaf Gluing Lemma establishes that overlaps contain nontrivial concealments, preventing global reconstruction from a single patch.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The Conservation Lemma shows that conserved quantities cannot be localized without boundary fluxes.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
Together, these imply that no single local perspective determines the plenum. 
Only the colimit of the covering family provides equivalence to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
Therefore, viewpoint diversity is enforced structurally by RSVP dynamics. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Scarcity of Interpretation]
Since entropy conceals below thresholds and prevents reconstruction from single domains, interpretations of the plenum are scarce by theorem. 
Scarcity is a categorical invariant: it arises from gluing constraints, not from sociological choice. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Concealment–Broadcast Duality]
Concealment (suppression of local observables) and broadcast (global downtree revelation) form an adjoint pair of functors
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dashv</span></span><span> B : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enforcing concealment and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enforcing revelation. 
Their tension generates diversity of perspectives. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Interpretive Note.} 
This theorem is the formal core of the monograph: the algebraic necessity of viewpoint diversity. 
It links the scriptural injunctions to conceal and preserve, the algebraic balancing of Al-Khwarizmi, the modular triggers of computation, and the probabilistic broadcast of the Conscious Turing Machine. 
In all cases, concealment prevents singular dominance and ensures multiplicity of perspectives. 
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
