01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{8.1 Formal Proof of the Concealment–Broadcast Adjunction}

We now expand Corollary 8.2 into a categorical proof, showing that concealment (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) and broadcast (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) form an adjoint pair. 

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Concealment Functor]
Define </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T(X) = ( </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-S}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> S),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
i.e., </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> acts by suppressing entropy contributions below thresholds. On morphisms </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f:X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> Y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T(f)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the same transition but post-composed with concealment scaling. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Broadcast Functor]
Define </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
B(X) = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
i.e., </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> acts by amplifying entropy into a global downtree broadcast. On morphisms </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f:X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> Y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B(f)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> extends </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with global broadcast overlays. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Adjunction of Concealment and Broadcast]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dashv</span></span><span> B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
We must provide natural bijections
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{Hom}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}}(T(X),Y) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{Hom}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}}(X,B(Y))
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
for all objects </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X,Y </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RSVP}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, natural in both </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Unit.} Define </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>X: X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> B(T(X))</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as the natural inclusion of a concealed state into its broadcast. Explicitly,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>X(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S) = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-S}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> S + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Counit.} Define </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>Y: T(B(Y)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> Y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as the natural retraction of a broadcast-then-concealed state back to its base. Explicitly,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>Y(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> (S + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}) e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-(S + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v})}) = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Triangle Identities.}  
For each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>X)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> followed by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{T(X)}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> reduces to the identity on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T(X)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> because concealment composed with concealment-broadcast-concealment is idempotent.  
For each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{B(Y)}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> followed by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>Y)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> reduces to the identity on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B(Y)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> because broadcasting then retracting stabilizes under broadcast overlay.

Thus </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dashv</span></span><span> B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> holds. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Dialectical Necessity]
Every RSVP evolution factors through the adjunction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dashv</span></span><span> B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, ensuring that concealment and revelation are structurally intertwined. 
This categorical dialectic enforces that no broadcast occurs without prior concealment, and no concealment persists without eventual broadcast. 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
