</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}[Proof sketch]
With </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> compact and the semiflow continuous, Krylov–Bogolyubov procedures (or Poincar</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\'</span></span><span>e recurrence for measure-preserving reductions) ensure recurrence to neighborhoods of omega-limit sets; Proposition~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:omega} identifies these as crystalline. The induced return map to a small </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-ball around </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is recurrent.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.5 Improbability of Bounces}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Bounce configuration]
A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{bounce} is a trajectory segment on which </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> jointly re-amplify from arbitrarily small neighborhoods to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> timescales, in the absence of external forcing.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Non-genericity of bounces]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{prop:bounce}
Under </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eqref</span></span><span>{eq:rsvp-appendix}, crystalline neighborhoods are locally asymptotically stable in the sense of Lyapunov: there exists </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2} &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> at </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then the same remains </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>&lt; 2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, with decay to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> along a subsequence. Consequently, bounces (as defined) require codimension-</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> tuning of initial data and are of measure zero in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>; they are dynamically negligible.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}[Proof sketch]
Linearize </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eqref</span></span><span>{eq:rsvp-appendix} about a crystalline state; the linearized operator has non-positive spectrum due to the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> dissipation and vanishing forcing, giving local asymptotic stability. Nonlinearities are controlled by smallness (a standard bootstrap).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.6 Conclusion}

Lemmas~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{lem:S-L1-decay}–</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{lem:v-uniform} establish entropy decay and spatial uniformization; Proposition~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:omega} shows crystalline omega-limits; Theorem~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{thm:reset} provides Poincar</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\'</span></span><span>e-type recurrences to crystalline neighborhoods under compactness; Proposition~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:bounce} demonstrates the non-genericity of bounces. Together these yield the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Crystalline Reset}: the RSVP flow tends to spatially homogeneous, low-entropy configurations and recurrently returns to them, making bounces vanishingly unlikely in the invariant phase set.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
