l gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="298">Here’s a <strong data-start="9" data-end="73">detailed outline of the full monograph <em data-start="50" data-end="71">Viewpoint Diversity</em></strong> as we’ve collaboratively built it, integrating all the themes you developed (biblical exegesis, algebra, perceptual control theory, active inference, CTM, RSVP, cosmological natural selection, and computational metaphors).</p>
<hr data-start="300" data-end="303">
<h1 data-start="305" data-end="351"><strong data-start="307" data-end="351">Monograph Outline: <em data-start="328" data-end="349">Viewpoint Diversity</em></strong></h1>
<hr data-start="353" data-end="356">
<h2 data-start="358" data-end="377"><strong data-start="361" data-end="377">Front Matter</strong></h2>
<ul data-start="378" data-end="459">
<li data-start="378" data-end="392">
<p data-start="380" data-end="392">Title Page</p>
</li>
<li data-start="393" data-end="405">
<p data-start="395" data-end="405">Abstract</p>
</li>
<li data-start="406" data-end="437">
<p data-start="408" data-end="437">Preface and Acknowledgments</p>
</li>
<li data-start="438" data-end="459">
<p data-start="440" data-end="459">Table of Contents</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="461" data-end="464">
<h2 data-start="466" data-end="512"><strong data-start="469" data-end="512">Part I: Ancient Roots of Interpretation</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="514" data-end="570"><strong data-start="518" data-end="570">Chapter 1. Job 31:33 and the First Written Verse</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="571" data-end="845">
<li data-start="571" data-end="634">
<p data-start="573" data-end="634">Argument that Job 31:33 is the earliest written injunction.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="635" data-end="723">
<p data-start="637" data-end="723">Original translation and commentary: concealment, covering, and scribal instruction.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="724" data-end="781">
<p data-start="726" data-end="781">Double reading: conceal excrement vs conceal meaning.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="782" data-end="845">
<p data-start="784" data-end="845">Scarcity of interpretation as a cultural survival strategy.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="847" data-end="895"><strong data-start="851" data-end="895">Chapter 2. The Seven Meanings of <em data-start="886" data-end="893">Iqraʾ</em></strong></h3>
<ul data-start="896" data-end="1184">
<li data-start="896" data-end="929">
<p data-start="898" data-end="929">Philological study of <em data-start="920" data-end="926">أقرأ</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="930" data-end="1064">
<p data-start="932" data-end="1064">Seven senses: read/study, rhyme/versify, draw near/return, delay/hold back, pregnancy, menstruation, monthly waiting, and warning.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1065" data-end="1130">
<p data-start="1067" data-end="1130">Implications for scriptural polysemy and epistemic restraint.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1131" data-end="1184">
<p data-start="1133" data-end="1184">Poetic reflections: <em data-start="1153" data-end="1181">The Seven Meanings of Read</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="1186" data-end="1252"><strong data-start="1190" data-end="1252">Chapter 3. Joseph, Samson, and Escape by Shedding Identity</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1253" data-end="1525">
<li data-start="1253" data-end="1327">
<p data-start="1255" data-end="1327">Joseph slipping away from Potiphar’s wife as lizard dropping its tail.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1328" data-end="1372">
<p data-start="1330" data-end="1372">Samson’s hair as a social-access marker.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1373" data-end="1449">
<p data-start="1375" data-end="1449">Escape narratives (Gethsemane, triple-diversion) as identity divestment.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1450" data-end="1525">
<p data-start="1452" data-end="1525">Theological and evolutionary functions of counterfoil identity markers.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1527" data-end="1530">
<h2 data-start="1532" data-end="1587"><strong data-start="1535" data-end="1587">Part II: Algebra, Balance, and Early Computation</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="1589" data-end="1642"><strong data-start="1593" data-end="1642">Chapter 4. Al-Jabr and the Fulcrum of Balance</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1643" data-end="1899">
<li data-start="1643" data-end="1715">
<p data-start="1645" data-end="1715">Al-Khwarizmi’s <em data-start="1660" data-end="1683">al-jabr wa-l-muqabala</em> as restoration and balancing.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1716" data-end="1771">
<p data-start="1718" data-end="1771">Original medical/bone-setting meaning of <em data-start="1759" data-end="1768">al-jabr</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1772" data-end="1834">
<p data-start="1774" data-end="1834">Equality as a fulcrum: comparison of weights on a balance.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1835" data-end="1899">
<p data-start="1837" data-end="1899">Transition from physical repair to mathematical abstraction.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="1901" data-end="1948"><strong data-start="1905" data-end="1948">Chapter 5. Weights, Graphs, and Neurons</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1949" data-end="2157">
<li data-start="1949" data-end="1998">
<p data-start="1951" data-end="1998">Linear equations as weight–balance relations.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1999" data-end="2067">
<p data-start="2001" data-end="2067">Graph-theoretic interpretations: cyclic vs acyclic connectivity.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2068" data-end="2119">
<p data-start="2070" data-end="2119">Neuron model: weight = slope, bias = intercept.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2120" data-end="2157">
<p data-start="2122" data-end="2157">Links to learning and plasticity.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2159" data-end="2162">
<h2 data-start="2164" data-end="2219"><strong data-start="2167" data-end="2219">Part III: Perception, Control, and Consciousness</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="2221" data-end="2281"><strong data-start="2225" data-end="2281">Chapter 6. Parable of the Sower as Natural Selection</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="2282" data-end="2467">
<li data-start="2282" data-end="2328">
<p data-start="2284" data-end="2328">Seeds as replicators; soil as environment.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2329" data-end="2394">
<p data-start="2331" data-end="2394">Readings across evolutionary, cognitive, and furtive domains.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2395" data-end="2467">
<p data-start="2397" data-end="2467">Parable as proto-description of variation, selection, and retention.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="2469" data-end="2534"><strong data-start="2473" data-end="2534">Chapter 7. Perceptual Control Theory and Active Inference</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="2535" data-end="2781">
<li data-start="2535" data-end="2587">
<p data-start="2537" data-end="2587">Powers’s PCT: behavior as control of perception.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2588" data-end="2659">
<p data-start="2590" data-end="2659">Friston’s free-energy principle: prediction and error minimization.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2660" data-end="2715">
